{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Test` - the runner's own vocabulary, as far as Design-Test.md §11 needs it.
   ==========================================================================================

   **This is F1's half of the library and not the framework.** Design-Test.md stages the vocabulary
   as Phase 0 - `check`/`require` in both moods, sections, snapshots, `agree`, `forAll` - and §11 is
   the *compiler* work those are written against. What is here is exactly what the synthesized entry
   names and nothing more: the four types a `Case` is made of, and the function the entry hands its
   cases to. Everything else in §4 belongs to Phase 0 and is deliberately absent rather than sketched.

   The shapes are §4.1's, unchanged:

       pub data Site {file: String, line: U32, column: U32, function: String}
       pub data Failure {site: Site, claim: String, detail: String}
       pub type Test = Result(Failure, {})
       pub data Case {name: String, moduleName: String, site: Site, aborts: Bool, body: () -> Test}

   `Site`'s strings are constants. A string literal is a borrowed run over a constant global and its
   teardown is nothing at all, so a `Site` costs some constant data and no runtime work - which is
   what makes it free to put one on every case and, through `@caller`, on every assertion.
-}

{-
   Where something is, filled by the compiler.

   The field *names* are the contract rather than the type: `@caller` matches a location record by
   name against a fixed vocabulary - `file` and `function` are text, `line` and `column` are numbers -
   because the compiler knows nothing about this declaration in particular. A test framework that
   wanted only the line would declare a record with only `line` in it and get one.
-}
pub data Site {file: String, line: Int, column: Int, function: String}

-- One claim that did not hold. `detail` is the second line a report gets - a hexdump, a diff - and is
-- empty for the assertions that have nothing to add.
pub data Failure {site: Site, claim: String, detail: String}

{-
   What a case answers.

   `Result` and not a type of its own, so that `?` works on it out of the box: `Core`'s `Try` class
   already gives `requireEqual(...)?` its meaning, and a test that returns early at the first thing
   that did not hold is that operator and nothing else.
-}
pub alias Test = Result(Failure, {})

{-
   One case, as the compiler hands it over.

   Everything here is known at the declaration and nothing is discovered at run time, which is the
   whole argument for the entry being synthesized: a registration list would carry the same five
   fields and would be missing for whichever test somebody forgot to add to it.
-}
pub data Case {name: String, moduleName: String, site: Site, aborts: Bool, body: () -> Test}

{-
   The runner - what the synthesized entry calls, and the program's status.

   Deliberately the plainest thing that reports: one line per case on the way past, a count at the
   end, and 0 or 1. The report *stream* §5 specifies, the sharding of §6 and the harness of §11.2's
   F4 all sit above this and none of them is what F1 is about.

   **A run that discovered no cases is a failure**, which is `test/README.md`'s empty-corpus guard
   said in the new framework's terms: a driver that verified nothing must not be mistaken for one
   that verified everything, and a test binary that ran zero cases is exactly that mistake.
-}
pub fn runMain(cases: [Case]) -> Int:
    -- As an `Int`, because that is the width `Show` covers and a count of cases is not a `Size`
    -- question. `length` answers the container width; the report reads a number.
    let count = truncate(length(cases)) :: Int

    if count == 0:
        print("no tests: a build that discovered no cases is a failure, not a pass\n")
        return 1

    let &failed = 0 :: Int
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < count:
        let case = cases[i :: Size]
        i = i + 1

        -- `aborts` cases need a child process to run in and a status to read back, which is F4's
        -- harness. Reported as skipped rather than run here, because running one in-process would
        -- take the whole suite down with it.
        if case.aborts:
            print("skip {case.moduleName}.{case.name} (@test(aborts) needs the harness)\n")
        else:
            match case.body():
                Ok(_):
                    print("pass {case.moduleName}.{case.name}\n")

                Err(failure):
                    failed = failed + 1
                    print("FAIL {case.moduleName}.{case.name}\n")
                    print("  {failure.site.file}:{failure.site.line}:{failure.site.column} {failure.claim}\n")

                    if length(failure.detail) != (0 :: Size):
                        print("  {failure.detail}\n")

    if failed == 0:
        print("{count} passed\n")
        return 0

    print("{failed} of {count} failed\n")
    return 1
